5 #edb/ref 17592186494671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494997 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494997 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494997 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494997 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab5b3c9e-bbb0-559d-a773-6cdda5c1c612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495076 #edb/ref 1071 "If $X=\\{x,y\\},$ then $P(X)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c03ab7a-4491-5a50-9d10-a9d4303200d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495076 #edb/ref 1074 "The power set of $X,$ denoted $P(X),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $X,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nSo, let's list all possible subsets of $X$ using a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/be/bea8536b726a48046b995d180bd9136146d491721a9197f696c7a5e9af43a6ba.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram, the subsets of $X$ are\n\n$$\n∅=\\{\\},\\,\\{x\\},\\,\\{y\\},\\,\\{x,y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(X)=\\{∅,\\{x\\},\\{y\\},\\{x,y\\}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495098 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495098 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{4,\\{∅\\}\\},$ then $P(A)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "704dcf7a-18fa-5b3e-a4d5-711d349f8966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495098 #edb/ref 1074 "The power set of $A,$ denoted $P(A),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $A,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nSo, let's list all possible subsets of $A$ using a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/05/05aba1d74ce21be5c12c7f72b0e5e9f5862b2a8ee67436f18fad1da1542bbc11.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram, the subsets of $A$ are\n\n$$\n∅=\\{\\},\\,\\{\\{∅\\}\\},\\,\\{4\\},\\,\\{4,\\{∅\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\nP(A)=\\{∅,\\{4\\},\\{\\{∅\\}\\},\\{4,\\{∅\\}\\}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495108 #edb/ref 1071 "If $Y=\\{\\{∅\\},5,\\{1\\}\\}$, then $|P(Y)|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89c6e0d5-6929-5303-908c-02dab60ff0bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495108 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $Y,$ we have $|P(Y)|={2}^{|Y|}.$ \n\nIn this case, $|Y|=3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n|P(Y)|={2}^{|Y|}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\{∅\\}$ is a single element in the set $X,$ and the same is true for $\\{1\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495126 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495126 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495126 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495126 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{a,b\\}$ and $B=\\{-1,0,1\\}$ then $|P(A)\\times P(B)|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495126 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae77173a-2ca2-505c-a9f6-aa510463577a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495126 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $S,$ we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ Also, $|S\\times T|=|S|\\cdot |T|.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n|A|=2,\\,|B|=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(A)| & ={2}^{|A|}={2}^{2}=4, \\\\ |P(B)| & ={2}^{|B|}={2}^{3}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(A)\\times P(B)| & =|P(A)|\\cdot |P(B)| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495134 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495134 #edb/ref 1071 "If $D=\\{-1,1\\},$ then $|P(D)|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b86efe0a-54ca-5d2e-8b08-c543ca6f9fa1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495134 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $D,$ we have $|P(D)|={2}^{|D|}.$ \n\nIn this case, $|D|=2,$ so we have\n\n$$\n|P(D)|={2}^{|D|}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495138 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495138 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495138 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495138 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(∅)=\\{∅\\}$\n\nII. $P(a)=\\{∅,\\{a\\}\\}$\n\nIII. $P(\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\})=\\{∅,\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495138 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c09d0cf0-247f-5da8-86ac-5074f5239c65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495138 #edb/ref 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set $∅$ is $\\{∅\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $a$ is not a set. So, the notation $P(a)$ does not make sense.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{∅,\\{∅\\},\\{\\{∅\\}\\},\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495159 #edb/ref 1071 "If $K=\\{3,k,9\\},$ then $|P(K)|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7e6e44c-b5ac-5425-8892-5340b5b344d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495159 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $K,$ we have $|P(K)|={2}^{|K|}.$ \n\nIn this case, $|K|=3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n|P(K)|={2}^{|K|}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
